## Tag `feature` — one feature on the target

The target's core is built. Its own worker ran its checks; nothing has
checked it since. Your task adds one feature (one of `task.yaml`'s
`features`), or makes the target serve one configuration variant as well.
**Before you change anything, boot the core on your worktree** at its
narrowest member (`trtllm-modeling-v2 gate boot --unit core ...`). If that
fails, do not build on it: end `BLOCKED` with the command and its result
line, so the core is fixed first. **Every check the target passed keeps
passing**: with the feature off, the target runs as it did. The checks your
dispatch lists say what the feature must pass. A feature that needs an entry the
vocabulary lacks blocks as a `vocabulary_gap`, as the core would. **Load the
`trtllm-modeling-v2-features` skill first**: where the feature lives in the
engine, what the target must honour for it, and how to prove it from a CPU
check up to its gate.
